<span class="ltx_bibblock"><em id="bib.bib35.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Nucleic acids research</em>, 52(D1):D1180–D1192, 2024.

</span></li>
    
</ul>
</section>
<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Details on DemoDiff</h2>

<section id="A1.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.1 </span>Graph-level Tokens and Pretraining Loss</h3>

<div id="A1.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Graph DiTs define a graph-level token that concatenates the node feature with all related edge features. In molecular generation, we use a special type of edge, the null edge, to represent that there is no edge between two nodes. Thus,
The feature vector <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}</annotation></semantics></math> (or <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}^{0}</annotation></semantics></math>) of a graph-level token
<math id="A1.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x=\{M,\{e_{j}\}_{j=1}^{d}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>d</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x=\{M,\{e_{j}\}_{j=1}^{d}\}</annotation></semantics></math> consists of three components:
<math id="A1.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\mathrm{motif}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>motif</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\mathrm{motif}}</annotation></semantics></math> motif types, <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\mathrm{bond}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>bond</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\mathrm{bond}}</annotation></semantics></math> bond types,
and <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\mathrm{attach}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>attach</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\mathrm{attach}}</annotation></semantics></math> attachment specifications.
Here, <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\mathrm{motif}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>motif</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\mathrm{motif}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the size of the motif vocabulary <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="A1.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\mathrm{bond}}=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>bond</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\mathrm{bond}}=4</annotation></semantics></math> represents null, single, double, and triple bonds,
and <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\mathrm{attach}}=\arg\max_{M\in\mathcal{M}}|M|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>attach</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>max</mi><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\mathrm{attach}}=\arg\max_{M\in\mathcal{M}}|M|</annotation></semantics></math> is the maximum number of atoms in <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math>. <a href="#S3.E3" title="In 3.2 In-Context Learning with Graph Diffusion Transformers ‣ 3 Learning Diffusion Model with Demonstrations ‣ Graph Diffusion Transformers are In-Context Molecular Designers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Eq.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> can be decomposed as
<math id="A1.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{pretrain}}=\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{motif}}+\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{bond}}+\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{attach}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>pretrain</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>motif</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>bond</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>attach</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{pretrain}}=\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{motif}}+\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{bond}}+\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{attach}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Specifically,</p>
<table id="A1.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A1.Ex1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{\text{pretrain}}=\mathbb{E}_{q(\mathbf{x})}\,\mathbb{E}_{q(\mathbf{x}^{t}\mid\mathbf{x})}\Bigg[" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>pretrain</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo maxsize="2.600em" minsize="2.600em">[</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{\text{pretrain}}=\mathbb{E}_{q(\mathbf{x})}\,\mathbb{E}_{q(\mathbf{x}^{t}\mid\mathbf{x})}\Bigg[</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\underbrace{-\log p_{\theta}^{\mathrm{motif}}\!\big(\mathbf{m}\mid\mathbf{x}^{t},\mathcal{C},Y\big)}_{\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{motif}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">−</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi><mi>motif</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mi>𝐦</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>motif</mi></msub></munder><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\underbrace{-\log p_{\theta}^{\mathrm{motif}}\!\big(\mathbf{m}\mid\mathbf{x}^{t},\mathcal{C},Y\big)}_{\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{motif}}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A1.Ex2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\underbrace{-\sum_{j=1}^{d}\log p_{\theta}^{\mathrm{bond}}\!\big(\mathbf{b}_{j}\mid\mathbf{x}^{t},\mathcal{C},Y\big)}_{\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{bond}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo></mstyle><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>d</mi></msup><mi>log</mi><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>θ</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>bond</mi></msup><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mi>𝐛</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>bond</mi></msub></munder><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\underbrace{-\sum_{j=1}^{d}\log p_{\theta}^{\mathrm{bond}}\!\big(\mathbf{b}_{j}\mid\mathbf{x}^{t},\mathcal{C},Y\big)}_{\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{bond}}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A1.E4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E4.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\underbrace{-\sum_{j=1}^{d}\log p_{\theta}^{\mathrm{attach}}\!\big(\mathbf{a}_{j}\mid\mathbf{x}^{t},\mathcal{C},Y\big)}_{\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{attach}}}\Bigg]." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo></mstyle><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>d</mi></msup><mi>log</mi><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>θ</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>attach</mi></msup><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mi>𝐚</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>attach</mi></msub></munder><mo maxsize="2.600em" minsize="2.600em">]</mo><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\underbrace{-\sum_{j=1}^{d}\log p_{\theta}^{\mathrm{attach}}\!\big(\mathbf{a}_{j}\mid\mathbf{x}^{t},\mathcal{C},Y\big)}_{\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{attach}}}\Bigg].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">To align feature dimensions across tokens, we use the dense edge representation by treating all non-connections as null bonds.
The resulting feature dimension is <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F=F_{\mathrm{motif}}+n\times F_{\mathrm{bond}}+n\times F_{\mathrm{attach}}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>motif</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>bond</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>attach</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F=F_{\mathrm{motif}}+n\times F_{\mathrm{bond}}+n\times F_{\mathrm{attach}},</annotation></semantics></math>
where <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> is the maximum number of nodes in the motif-represented dataset.
For optimization with <a href="#A1.Ex1" title="In A.1 Graph-level Tokens and Pretraining Loss ‣ Appendix A Details on DemoDiff ‣ Graph Diffusion Transformers are In-Context Molecular Designers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, we include the null bond type in <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{bond}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>bond</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{bond}}</annotation></semantics></math> but exclude attachment specifications of null edges in <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{attach}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>attach</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{attach}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.2 </span>Transition Matrices in Diffusion Models</h3>

<div id="A1.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We define the transition matrix <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐐</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> that perturbs molecules at the motif level to pretrain DemoDiff.
We model the joint distribution of nodes and edges with the transition matrix. It is constructed from four submatrices <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Q}_{V},\mathbf{Q}_{EV},\mathbf{Q}_{E},\mathbf{Q}_{VE}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐐</mi><mi>V</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐐</mi><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐐</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐐</mi><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Q}_{V},\mathbf{Q}_{EV},\mathbf{Q}_{E},\mathbf{Q}_{VE}</annotation></semantics></math>,
denoting transitions <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{node}\to\text{node}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>node</mtext><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mtext>node</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{node}\to\text{node}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{edge}\to\text{node}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>edge</mtext><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mtext>node</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{edge}\to\text{node}</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="A1.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{edge}\to\text{edge}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>edge</mtext><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mtext>edge</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{edge}\to\text{edge}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{node}\to\text{edge}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>node</mtext><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mtext>edge</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{node}\to\text{edge}</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively:</p>
<table id="A1.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Q}_{G}=\begin{bmatrix}\mathbf{Q}_{V}&amp;\mathbf{1}_{N}^{\prime}\otimes\mathbf{Q}_{VE}\\
\mathbf{1}_{n}\otimes\mathbf{Q}_{EV}&amp;\mathbf{1}_{n\times n}\otimes\mathbf{Q}_{E}\end{bmatrix}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐐</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>𝐐</mi><mi>V</mi></msub></mtd><mtd><mrow><msubsup><mn>𝟏</mn><mi>N</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>𝐐</mi><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>𝐐</mi><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mtd><mtd><mrow><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>𝐐</mi><mi>E</mi></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Q}_{G}=\begin{bmatrix}\mathbf{Q}_{V}&amp;\mathbf{1}_{N}^{\prime}\otimes\mathbf{Q}_{VE}\\
\mathbf{1}_{n}\otimes\mathbf{Q}_{EV}&amp;\mathbf{1}_{n\times n}\otimes\mathbf{Q}_{E}\end{bmatrix},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\otimes" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⊗</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\otimes</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the Kronecker product, and <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{1}_{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mi>N</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{1}_{N}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{1}_{n}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mn>𝟏</mn><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{1}_{n}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>,
and <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{1}_{n\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{1}_{n\times n}</annotation></semantics></math> are the column vector, row vector, and all-ones matrix, respectively.
Here <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of nodes. For edges, diffusion is applied to bond types only,
while attachment attributes are optimized and predicted directly by the denoising model
as in <a href="#A1.Ex1" title="In A.1 Graph-level Tokens and Pretraining Loss ‣ Appendix A Details on DemoDiff ‣ Graph Diffusion Transformers are In-Context Molecular Designers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>. For categorical sampling, we separate the unnormalized logits of node and edge from the model outputs, compute probabilities for each motif and bond individually before sampling.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To obtain the transition matrices, we use the prior from the pretraining data.
The noisy distribution is defined as the marginal distributions of motif types <math id="A1.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{m}_{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐦</mi><mi>V</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{m}_{V}</annotation></semantics></math>
and bond types <math id="A1.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{m}_{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐦</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{m}_{E}</annotation></semantics></math>. The transition matrices are defined as
<math id="A1.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Q}_{V}=\bar{\alpha}^{t}\mathbf{I}+(1-\bar{\alpha}^{t})\mathbf{1}\mathbf{m}_{V}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐐</mi><mi>V</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐈</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>𝟏</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐦</mi><mi>V</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Q}_{V}=\bar{\alpha}^{t}\mathbf{I}+(1-\bar{\alpha}^{t})\mathbf{1}\mathbf{m}_{V}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>
and
<math id="A1.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Q}_{E}=\bar{\alpha}^{t}\mathbf{I}+(1-\bar{\alpha}^{t})\mathbf{1}\mathbf{m}_{E}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐐</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐈</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>𝟏</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐦</mi><mi>E</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Q}_{E}=\bar{\alpha}^{t}\mathbf{I}+(1-\bar{\alpha}^{t})\mathbf{1}\mathbf{m}_{E}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>,
where <math id="A1.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{m}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐦</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{m}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the transpose and <math id="A1.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐈</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{I}</annotation></semantics></math> is the identity matrix.
We compute co-occurrence frequencies of motif and bond types in training graphs
to obtain the marginal distributions <math id="A1.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{m}_{EV}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐦</mi><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{m}_{EV}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{m}_{VE}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐦</mi><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{m}_{VE}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Each row in <math id="A1.SS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{m}_{EV}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐦</mi><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{m}_{EV}</annotation></semantics></math> gives the probability of co-occurring motifs for a bond type,
and <math id="A1.SS2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{m}_{VE}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐦</mi><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{m}_{VE}</annotation></semantics></math> is its transpose. The transition matrices are then defined as
<math id="A1.SS2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Q}_{EV}=\bar{\alpha}^{t}\mathbf{I}+(1-\bar{\alpha}^{t})\mathbf{1}\mathbf{m}_{EV}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐐</mi><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐈</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>𝟏</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐦</mi><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Q}_{EV}=\bar{\alpha}^{t}\mathbf{I}+(1-\bar{\alpha}^{t})\mathbf{1}\mathbf{m}_{EV}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>
and
<math id="A1.SS2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Q}_{VE}=\bar{\alpha}^{t}\mathbf{I}+(1-\bar{\alpha}^{t})\mathbf{1}\mathbf{m}_{VE}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐐</mi><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐈</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>𝟏</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐦</mi><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Q}_{VE}=\bar{\alpha}^{t}\mathbf{I}+(1-\bar{\alpha}^{t})\mathbf{1}\mathbf{m}_{VE}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="A1.SS2.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\alpha}^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\alpha}^{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is cumulative noise coefficient in diffusion. The cosine schedule is chosen as <math id="A1.SS2.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\alpha}^{t}=\operatorname{cos}(0.5\pi(t/T+s)/(1+s))^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>0.5</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\alpha}^{t}=\operatorname{cos}(0.5\pi(t/T+s)/(1+s))^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.3 </span>Details on Consistency Score</h3>

<div id="A1.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given a query <math id="A1.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>, demonstrations <math id="A1.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}_{i}=\{(X_{ij},Y_{ij})\}_{j=1}^{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>L</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}_{i}=\{(X_{ij},Y_{ij})\}_{j=1}^{L}</annotation></semantics></math> are divided into positive <math id="A1.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}^{\mathrm{pos}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>pos</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}^{\mathrm{pos}}</annotation></semantics></math>, medium <math id="A1.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}^{\mathrm{med}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>med</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}^{\mathrm{med}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and negative <math id="A1.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}^{\mathrm{neg}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>neg</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}^{\mathrm{neg}}</annotation></semantics></math> examples to guide ICL. For a generated molecule <math id="A1.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, we use the Tanimoto similarity of fingerprints as the similarity measure. We compute the similarity between <math id="A1.SS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> and all molecules in each group and average them to obtain group-wise similarity scores</p>
<table id="A1.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{sim}^{\mathrm{pos}},\;\mathrm{sim}^{\mathrm{med}},\;\mathrm{sim}^{\mathrm{neg}}\in[0,1]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>sim</mi><mi>pos</mi></msup><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><msup><mi>sim</mi><mi>med</mi></msup><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>sim</mi><mi>neg</mi></msup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{sim}^{\mathrm{pos}},\;\mathrm{sim}^{\mathrm{med}},\;\mathrm{sim}^{\mathrm{neg}}\in[0,1].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Difference-based score.</span>
We compute margin differences between groups:</p>
<table id="A1.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\mathrm{pos,med}}=\max(\mathrm{sim}^{\mathrm{pos}}-\mathrm{sim}^{\mathrm{med}},0),\quad d_{\mathrm{med,neg}}=\max(\mathrm{sim}^{\mathrm{med}}-\mathrm{sim}^{\mathrm{neg}},0)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>pos</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>med</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>sim</mi><mi>pos</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>sim</mi><mi>med</mi></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>med</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>neg</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>sim</mi><mi>med</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>sim</mi><mi>neg</mi></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\mathrm{pos,med}}=\max(\mathrm{sim}^{\mathrm{pos}}-\mathrm{sim}^{\mathrm{med}},0),\quad d_{\mathrm{med,neg}}=\max(\mathrm{sim}^{\mathrm{med}}-\mathrm{sim}^{\mathrm{neg}},0),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="A1.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\mathrm{pos,neg}}=\max(\mathrm{sim}^{\mathrm{pos}}-\mathrm{sim}^{\mathrm{neg}},0)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>pos</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>neg</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>sim</mi><mi>pos</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>sim</mi><mi>neg</mi></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\mathrm{pos,neg}}=\max(\mathrm{sim}^{\mathrm{pos}}-\mathrm{sim}^{\mathrm{neg}},0).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">The normalized difference-based score is</p>
<table id="A1.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{\mathrm{diff}}=\min\left(\frac{d_{\mathrm{pos,med}}+d_{\mathrm{med,neg}}+d_{\mathrm{pos,neg}}}{3},\,1\right)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>diff</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>pos</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>med</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>med</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>neg</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>pos</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>neg</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mn>3</mn></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mn> 1</mn><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{\mathrm{diff}}=\min\left(\frac{d_{\mathrm{pos,med}}+d_{\mathrm{med,neg}}+d_{\mathrm{pos,neg}}}{3},\,1\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In experiments, the consistency score can be computed efficiently before applying Oracle functions. For example, we generate 1000 molecules and select the top 100 with the highest consistency scores. These molecules better follow the order of structural similarity across positive, medium, and negative examples. This removes poor generations that conflict with the demonstration semantics and increases confidence that selected molecules align with the query scores. <a href="#A1.T3" title="In A.3 Details on Consistency Score ‣ Appendix A Details on DemoDiff ‣ Graph Diffusion Transformers are In-Context Molecular Designers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> reports empirical improvements across task categories, each containing 4–7 tasks (<a href="#A3.SS1" title="C.1 Details on Experimental Set-ups ‣ Appendix C Details on Experiments ‣ Graph Diffusion Transformers are In-Context Molecular Designers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.1</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<figure id="A1.T3" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 3: </span>Improvement with the consistency score (average Top-10 harmonic scores).</figcaption>
<table id="A1.T3.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="A1.T3.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A1.T3.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_tt">Category</td>
<td id="A1.T3.2.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt">Without</td>
<td id="A1.T3.2.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt">With</td>
<td id="A1.T3.2.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt">Improvement</td></tr>
<tr id="A1.T3.2.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A1.T3.2.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t">Drug Design</td>
<td id="A1.T3.2.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">0.6230</td>
<td id="A1.T3.2.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">0.7943</td>
<td id="A1.T3.2.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><math id="A1.T3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+27.5\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>27.5</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+27.5\%</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="A1.T3.2.3" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A1.T3.2.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">Drug MPO</td>
<td id="A1.T3.2.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">0.4592</td>
<td id="A1.T3.2.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">0.5400</td>
<td id="A1.T3.2.3.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.T3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+17.6\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>17.6</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+17.6\%</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="A1.T3.2.4" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A1.T3.2.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">Drug Rediscovery</td>
<td id="A1.T3.2.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">0.4110</td>
<td id="A1.T3.2.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">0.4407</td>
<td id="A1.T3.2.4.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.T3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+7.2\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>7.2</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+7.2\%</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="A1.T3.2.5" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A1.T3.2.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">Structure Constrained</td>
<td id="A1.T3.2.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">0.5258</td>
<td id="A1.T3.2.5.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">0.5598</td>
<td id="A1.T3.2.5.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.T3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+6.5\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>6.5</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+6.5\%</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="A1.T3.2.6" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A1.T3.2.6.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">Materials Design</td>
<td id="A1.T3.2.6.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">0.6407</td>
<td id="A1.T3.2.6.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">0.6724</td>
<td id="A1.T3.2.6.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.T3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+4.9\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>4.9</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+4.9\%</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="A1.T3.2.7" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A1.T3.2.7.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb">Target-Based</td>
<td id="A1.T3.2.7.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">0.7745</td>
<td id="A1.T3.2.7.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">0.7803</td>
<td id="A1.T3.2.7.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><math id="A1.T3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+0.8\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>0.8</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+0.8\%</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
</table>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>Details on Pretraining</h2>

<div id="A2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The final pretraining dataset contains 1,084,566 molecules (polymers) and 155,150 unique assays or properties, yielding 1,639,515 tasks. These are constructed from ChEMBL <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zdrazil et al., 2024</a>)</cite> and multiple polymer data sources <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Otsuka et al., 2011</a>; <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Thornton et al., 2012</a>; <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kuenneth et al., 2021</a>)</cite>. Each task has a query molecule–score pair with the query score fixed at 1. Up to 45 molecules are grouped into positive, medium, and negative demonstrations based on their scores. The query molecule is the target, while the query score and demonstrations serve as inputs to DemoDiff during pretraining on <a href="#S3.E3" title="In 3.2 In-Context Learning with Graph Diffusion Transformers ‣ 3 Learning Diffusion Model with Demonstrations ‣ Graph Diffusion Transformers are In-Context Molecular Designers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Eq.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>.
For pretraining with a fixed maximum context window, we allocate half the window to positive demonstrations and one quarter each to medium and negative demonstrations, after excluding the target molecule.</p>
</div>
<section id="A2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.1 </span>Pretraining dataset</h3>

<section id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">ChEMBL dataset</h4>

<div id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We constructed molecular activity contexts from the ChEMBL database (version 35), which provides a large collection of bioactivity measurements across diverse assays. ChEMBL standardizes published activity types, values, and units into a unified variable, <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\mathrm{ChEMBL}=-\log(\text{molar IC}{50},\text{XC}{50},\text{EC}{50},\text{AC}{50},K_{i},K_{d},\text{or Potency})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ChEMBL</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">−</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mtext>molar IC</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>50</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mtext>XC</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>50</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mtext>EC</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>50</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mtext>AC</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>50</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mtext>or Potency</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\mathrm{ChEMBL}=-\log(\text{molar IC}{50},\text{XC}{50},\text{EC}{50},\text{AC}{50},K_{i},K_{d},\text{or Potency})</annotation></semantics></math>. This value places different measures of half-maximal response, potency, or affinity on a comparable negative logarithmic scale. For example, an IC<span id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text">50</span> of 1 nanomolar (<math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\times 10^{-9}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\times 10^{-9}</annotation></semantics></math> M) corresponds to a <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\mathrm{ChEMBL}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ChEMBL</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\mathrm{ChEMBL}</annotation></semantics></math> value of 9.
We extracted assay-level activity values (<math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\mathrm{ChEMBL}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ChEMBL</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\mathrm{ChEMBL}</annotation></semantics></math>).
For each assay, molecules were grouped according to their recorded activities. Within each group, we selected anchor molecules with strong activity (<math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\mathrm{ChEMBL}&gt;6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ChEMBL</mi></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\mathrm{ChEMBL}&gt;6</annotation></semantics></math>) as targets for building demonstrations. Each anchor was compared against all other molecules in the same assay to compute normalized distances, defined as the relative difference between the anchor’s <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\mathrm{ChEMBL}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ChEMBL</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\mathrm{ChEMBL}</annotation></semantics></math> value and that of the candidate molecule, converted to the range <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>. Specifically, for an anchor with value <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{a}</annotation></semantics></math> and a candidate with value <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{c}</annotation></semantics></math>, the normalized distance was given by <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=(v_{1}-v_{c})/10." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=(v_{1}-v_{c})/10.</annotation></semantics></math>
Based on this distance, we partitioned candidate molecules into three categories relative to the anchor. Molecules with distances in <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,0.25)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.25</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,0.25)</annotation></semantics></math> correspond to candidates with activity between <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="75\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>75</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">75\%</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="100\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>100</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">100\%</annotation></semantics></math> of the anchor and were assigned to the positive context. Molecules with distances in <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0.25,0.5)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0.25</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.5</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0.25,0.5)</annotation></semantics></math> correspond to candidates with activity between <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="50\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>50</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">50\%</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="75\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>75</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">75\%</annotation></semantics></math> of the anchor and were assigned to the medium context. Molecules with distances <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0.5,1.0]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0.5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1.0</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0.5,1.0]</annotation></semantics></math> correspond to candidates with activity below <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="50\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>50</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">50\%</annotation></semantics></math> of the anchor and were assigned to the negative context. From each category, we sampled up to <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="15" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>15</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">15</annotation></semantics></math> molecules to balance neighborhood size. Thus, there are up to 45 demonstration molecules for each task. Not all of them are used during pretraining due to the constraint of maximum context length.
This procedure produced triplets of anchor molecules and their associated positive, medium, and negative contexts.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Polymeric materials</h4>

<div id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We have polymeric material datasets from different sources, including PolyInfo <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Otsuka et al., 2011</a>)</cite>, MSA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Thornton et al., 2012</a>)</cite>, and from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kuenneth et al., 2021</a>)</cite>.
We considered a wide range of polymer properties spanning several categories, including thermal properties (e.g., heat capacity, glass transition temperature, melting temperature, and thermal conductivity), electronic properties (e.g., ionization energy, electron affinity, and band gap), structural properties (e.g., density, crystallinity, and radius of gyration), and transport properties (e.g., gas diffusion, solubility, and permeability coefficients).
For each property, raw values were normalized to the unit interval using min–max scaling, with logarithmic transformation applied when dynamic ranges exceeded <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1000</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1000</annotation></semantics></math> and non-negative shifts applied when necessary. Each polymer with valid property values was treated as an anchor, and pairwise distances in normalized property space were computed against all other polymers. Candidate molecules were partitioned into positive <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,0.25)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.25</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,0.25)</annotation></semantics></math>, medium <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0.25,0.5)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0.25</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.5</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0.25,0.5)</annotation></semantics></math>, and negative <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0.5,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0.5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0.5,1]</annotation></semantics></math> contexts, with up to <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="15" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>15</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">15</annotation></semantics></math> examples sampled per category based on smallest distances.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.2 </span>Tokenizer Preparation</h3>

<figure id="alg1" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 1</span> </span> Node Pair Encoding (NPE) with Constraints</figcaption>
<div id="alg1.4" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg1.l0" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">0:</span>
              
              
             molecule list <math id="alg1.l0.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math>, motif vocabulary <math id="alg1.l0.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}=\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}=\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math>, max size <math id="alg1.l0.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>, ring count threshold <math id="alg1.l0.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{\text{ring}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mtext>ring</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{\text{ring}}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l0a" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">0:</span>
              
              
             motif vocabulary <math id="alg1.l0a.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">1:</span>
              
              
             Initialize each molecule <math id="alg1.l1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\in\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\in\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math> with atom-level and ring-based motifs

</div>
<div id="alg1.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">2:</span>
              
              
             Count frequencies of ring-based motifs across <math id="alg1.l2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l3" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">3:</span>
              
              
             Add all periodic-table elements, polymerization “*”, and top-<math id="alg1.l3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{\text{ring}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mtext>ring</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{\text{ring}}</annotation></semantics></math> frequent rings to <math id="alg1.l3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l4" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">4:</span>
              
              
             <span id="alg1.l4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">while</span> <math id="alg1.l4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathcal{M}|&lt;K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathcal{M}|&lt;K</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>



</div>
<div id="alg1.l5" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">5:</span>
              
              
              <span id="alg1.l5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(1) Merge Neighbor:</span> Initialize empty multiset <math id="alg1.l5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}\leftarrow\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}\leftarrow\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l6" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">6:</span>
              
              
              <span id="alg1.l6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> each molecule <math id="alg1.l6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\in\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\in\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>



</div>
<div id="alg1.l7" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">7:</span>
              
              
               <span id="alg1.l7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> each motif <math id="alg1.l7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="alg1.l7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>



</div>
<div id="alg1.l8" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">8:</span>
              
              
                <span id="alg1.l8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> each adjacent motif <math id="alg1.l8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="alg1.l8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="alg1.l8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="alg1.l8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> are mergeable under structural constraints (e.g., rings treated as units) <span id="alg1.l8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>



</div>
<div id="alg1.l9" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">9:</span>
              
              
                 form new motif <math id="alg1.l9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\leftarrow m\cup m^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∪</mo><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\leftarrow m\cup m^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l10" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">10:</span>
              
              
                 add <math id="alg1.l10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> to multiset <math id="alg1.l10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> with frequency count

</div>
<div id="alg1.l11" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">11:</span>
              
              
